# ============================================================
# Fluxa Turtle
# ============================================================

# --- Working notes, not part of the product ------------------
AGENTS.md
FLUXA_GAPS.md
*.pdf

# --- Reference project, not part of this code ----------------
# nave/ (The Starfight Rebellion) is here only as an example of
# the Fluxa programming pattern. It has its own repository.
nave/

# --- The runtime, built from the language repository ---------
# github.com/RodBarenco/fluxa-lang — a platform-specific binary
# does not belong in the source of an artwork tool. The README
# says how to build it.
/fluxa

# --- The language documentation, copied here to work offline -
# It belongs to the language repository and drifts from it.
fluxa-docs/

# --- Working renders -----------------------------------------
# docs/artworks/ is published (ARTWORKS.md shows those images).
# docs/obras/ is the scratch folder they came out of.
docs/obras/

# --- Export output -------------------------------------------
# Frames and videos are generated from the source code: what is
# worth versioning is the artwork (main.flx), not its render.
export/
export[0-9]*/
lab/frames/
*.mp4
*.webm
*.gif
# ...except the published artworks, which are part of the documentation
!docs/artworks/*.mp4

# --- Verification harness output -----------------------------
lab/*.png

# --- Source art that is not the project's to publish ----------
# A scan or a photo of your own drawing: it lives here so the tools can read
# it, and it stays out of the repository. The turtle code traced FROM it is
# yours to commit; the drawing is yours to keep.
tartaruga.png
art/
__pycache__/

# --- System --------------------------------------------------
core
core.*
*.core
.DS_Store
*.swp
*.log
.vscode/
.idea/
